.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt ELOG 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm elog
.Nd eventlog binary capture file format
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An
.Nm
file stores a captured sequence of kernel events produced by the
.Xr eventlog 9
framework.
Files are created by the
.Xr elog 1
utility using the
.Fl o
option and can be read back with the
.Fl r
option.
.Pp
All multi-byte integer fields are in host-native byte order
.Pq little-endian on amd64 and aarch64 .
.Pp
An
.Nm
file consists of the following contiguous sections:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[file header (36 bytes)]
[header counters (N*8 bytes)]
[provider table (4 + N*50-byte entries)]
[decode-blob section]      (optional)
[event stream]
.Ed
.Pp
The decode-blob section is optional (see the
.Sx Decode Blob Section
subsection).
.Ss File Header
The version 3 file header is 36 bytes, packed with no padding; version 2 is
40, with an 8-byte
.Va dropped_events
in place of the trailing
.Va counter_count :
.Bl -column "Offset" "Size" "uint64_t" "counter_count" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Size Ta Sy Type Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 4 Ta Vt char[4] Ta Va magic
.It 4 Ta 4 Ta Vt uint32_t Ta Va version
.It 8 Ta 8 Ta Vt uint64_t Ta Va capture_start
.It 16 Ta 8 Ta Vt uint64_t Ta Va start_utc_us
.It 24 Ta 8 Ta Vt uint64_t Ta Va event_count
.It 32 Ta 4 Ta Vt uint32_t Ta Va counter_count
.El
.Pp
The fields are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va magic
The four ASCII bytes
.Dq ELOG
.Pq Li 0x45 0x4C 0x4F 0x47 .
A consumer must verify this field before proceeding.
.It Va version
Format version number.
The current version, and the only one written, is 3: each provider table
entry carries a
.Va schema_id ,
an optional decode-blob section follows the provider table (see
.Sx Decode Blob Section ) ,
and the header carries the counter array described in
.Sx Header Counters .
Version 2 is identical except that the header is a fixed 40 bytes, with a
single
.Vt uint64_t
.Va dropped_events
counter in place of
.Va counter_count
and the counter array.
Because version 2 shipped in a release its on-disk layout cannot be changed
in place, which is why version 3 is a new version rather than a redefinition
of version 2.
Version 1 (historical: no
.Va schema_id ,
no decode-blob section, no counter array) is obsolete.
.Xr elog 1
reads version 2 and version 3 files and rejects version 1.
A consumer should reject any other version.
.It Va capture_start
Capture start, in microseconds since boot.
.It Va start_utc_us
UTC wall-clock time at capture start, in microseconds since the Unix epoch.
Used together with
.Va capture_start
to convert event timestamps to UTC:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
utc_us = start_utc_us + (event.timestamp - capture_start)
.Ed
.Pp
The two name the same instant, which is what makes that conversion hold.
An event may carry a timestamp below
.Va capture_start :
a user-space producer writes its identity record when it registers with
.Xr eventlog_user 3 ,
so a capture that drains a long-running provider's ring picks up records
from before it started.
Those convert to a UTC time before
.Va start_utc_us .
.It Va event_count
Total number of events in the file.
Written as 0 initially and updated by seeking back to the header when
capture ends.
May be 0 if the capturing tool was killed.
.It Va counter_count
Number of
.Vt uint64_t
counters immediately following the header; see
.Sx Header Counters .
.El
.Pp
The C structure is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct elog_binary_header {
	char     magic[4];
	uint32_t version;
	uint64_t capture_start;
	uint64_t start_utc_us;
	uint64_t event_count;
	uint32_t counter_count;
} __packed;
.Ed
.Pp
.Va counter_count
packed
.Vt uint64_t
values immediately follow the header; see
.Sx Header Counters .
.Ss Header Counters
Immediately after the file header,
.Va counter_count
packed
.Vt uint64_t
values record drop statistics as of the last time the header was
(re)written.
The array is indexed positionally and append-only: a given index's meaning
is fixed for the life of the format, new counters are only ever added at
the end, and a reader treats any index at or beyond the file's own
.Va counter_count
as unavailable
.Pq value 0
rather than an error.
This lets a newer
.Xr elog 1
read an older file that has fewer counters, and an older
.Xr elog 1
read a newer file with more, without a format-version change either way.
.Pp
The currently defined indices are:
.Bl -column "Index" "ELOG_COUNTER_USER_DROPPED_PREJOIN" -offset indent
.It Sy Index Ta Sy Name Ta Sy Meaning
.It 0 Ta Dv ELOG_COUNTER_KERNEL_DROPPED Ta Kernel per-CPU buffer-full drops
.It 1 Ta Dv ELOG_COUNTER_USER_DROPPED_FULL Ta User-stream buffer-full drops
.It 2 Ta Dv ELOG_COUNTER_USER_DROPPED_PREJOIN Ta User events older than the join point
.It 3 Ta Dv ELOG_COUNTER_USER_DROPPED_LATE Ta User events older than the stream's last delivered
.El
.Pp
.Dv ELOG_COUNTER_KERNEL_DROPPED
counts events the kernel dropped due to full per-CPU buffers during
capture, regardless of provider kind.
The remaining three counters apply only to user-space producers
.Pq see Xr eventlog_user 3 :
.Dv ELOG_COUNTER_USER_DROPPED_FULL
counts events dropped because a producer's kernel-side per-stream buffer
filled before the subscriber drained it;
.Dv ELOG_COUNTER_USER_DROPPED_PREJOIN
counts events older than the subscriber's join point, i.e. the producer's
stream first appeared mid-capture and some of its history predates the
subscription;
.Dv ELOG_COUNTER_USER_DROPPED_LATE
counts events whose timestamp is older than one already delivered for the
same stream, an ordering violation that usually indicates a producer bug.
.Pp
All counters are cumulative as of the last header write, not a true
per-file delta: when one capture spans several files
.Pq Fl o Cm dir= Ns Ar path No or Fl -roll- Ns Ar * ,
a drop that occurs while one file is open cannot be attributed to that
file alone if the same kernel subscriber later writes further files, since
the drop counts are shared across the whole subscription.
.Ss Provider Table
Immediately after the header counters, the provider table maps numeric
.Va provider_id
values found in event headers to human-readable provider names and to a
content-addressed
.Va schema_id .
.Pp
The table starts with a 4-byte count:
.Bl -column "Offset" "Size" "uint32_t" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Size Ta Sy Type Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 4 Ta Vt uint32_t Ta Va count
.El
.Pp
This is followed by
.Va count
packed 50-byte entries:
.Bl -column "Offset" "Size" "uint16_t" "provider_id" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Size Ta Sy Type Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 2 Ta Vt uint16_t Ta Va provider_id
.It 2 Ta 32 Ta Vt char[32] Ta Va name
.It 34 Ta 16 Ta Vt uint8_t[16] Ta Va schema_id
.El
.Pp
The
.Va name
field is null-terminated and zero-padded to 32 bytes.
Multiple entries may share the same name when multiple kernel subsystems
register providers under the same name
.Pq see Xr eventlog 9 .
The number of entries is not fixed: the capturing tool queries the kernel
for the full set of live providers, so the table grows to match.
.Pp
The
.Va schema_id
is the SHA-256 of the provider's decode blob truncated to 16 bytes
.Pq see Xr eventlog 9 ;
it is all-zero when the provider registered no decode blob.
It is the durable key that binds this capture's
.Va provider_id
values to a specific schema, so the file can be decoded later
.Pq even after a reboot reassigns Va provider_id Ns s
against the matching blob in the decode-blob section or the live kernel.
.Pp
The total provider table size is
.Li 4 + count * 50
bytes.
.Pp
The C structure for each entry is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct eventlog_provider_info {
	uint16_t provider_id;
	char     name[EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_NAME_MAX];
	uint8_t  schema_id[16];
} __packed;
.Ed
.Ss Decode Blob Section
This optional section carries the per-provider
.Dq decode blobs
(see
.Xr eventlog 9
and
.In sys/eventlog_decode.h )
that
.Xr elog 1
uses to decode events at runtime, so a version 3 file can be decoded on a host
that does not run the originating kernel.
.Pp
The section begins with a 4-byte count:
.Bl -column "Offset" "Size" "uint32_t" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Size Ta Sy Type Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 4 Ta Vt uint32_t Ta Va blob_count
.El
.Pp
A
.Va blob_count
of zero means no blobs were embedded
.Pq the file was written with Fl N ;
the section is then just the 4-byte count.
Otherwise
.Va blob_count
entries follow.
Blobs are deduplicated by
.Va schema_id ,
so each distinct schema appears once regardless of how many
.Va provider_id
values map to it.
Each entry consists of the 16-byte
.Va schema_id ,
a 4-byte blob size, and that many raw blob bytes:
.Bl -column "Size" "uint8_t[16]" "schema_id" -offset indent
.It Sy Size Ta Sy Type Ta Sy Field
.It 16 Ta Vt uint8_t[16] Ta Va schema_id
.It 4 Ta Vt uint32_t Ta Va blob_size
.It Va blob_size Ta Vt uint8_t[] Ta Va blob
.El
.Pp
The
.Va schema_id
matches a provider table entry's
.Va schema_id
.Pq the SHA-256-truncation of the blob bytes, see Sx Provider Table .
The
.Va blob
contents are described by
.In sys/eventlog_decode.h ;
its first four bytes are the ASCII magic
.Dq EDB1 .
When decoding a file
.Pq Xr elog 1 Fl r ,
each provider table entry is resolved by its
.Va schema_id :
the embedded blob is used if present, otherwise the blob is fetched from the
live kernel by
.Va schema_id ,
and decoding fails if neither source has it.
Version 1 files predate the
.Va schema_id
entirely and are no longer read.
Version 2 files carry the same provider table and decode-blob section as
version 3, differing only in the header
.Pq see Sx File Header ,
and are read normally.
.Ss Event Stream
The remainder of the file is a packed sequence of events in timestamp
order.
Each event consists of a 32-byte header followed by a variable-length
payload:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[header (32 bytes)][payload (0+ bytes)]
.Ed
.Pp
Events are packed back-to-back with no inter-event padding.
The event header layout is:
.Bl -column "Offset" "Size" "uint64_t" "event_length" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Size Ta Sy Type Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 2 Ta Vt uint16_t Ta Va event_length
.It 2 Ta 2 Ta Vt uint16_t Ta Va cpu
.It 4 Ta 2 Ta Vt uint16_t Ta Va provider_id
.It 6 Ta 2 Ta Vt uint16_t Ta (reserved)
.It 8 Ta 8 Ta Vt uint64_t Ta Va timestamp
.It 16 Ta 8 Ta Vt uint64_t Ta Va session_id
.It 24 Ta 4 Ta Vt uint32_t Ta Va event_id
.It 28 Ta 4 Ta Vt int32_t Ta Va thread_id
.El
.Pp
The
.Va event_length
field gives the total event size in bytes, including the header.
The minimum value is 32
.Pq header only, no payload ;
the maximum is 65535.
A parser advances to the next event by adding
.Va event_length
to the current event's offset.
.Pp
The
.Va provider_id
maps to a provider name via the provider table.
The
.Va timestamp
is in microseconds since boot; convert to UTC using the formula
described in the file header section.
The
.Va session_id
is provider-defined.
The reserved field at offset 6 is written as zero and must be ignored.
.Pp
Three
.Va event_id
values are reserved across all providers:
.Bl -column "EVENTLOG_SESSION_CREATE_ID" "0xFFFFFFFE" -offset indent
.It Sy Constant Ta Sy Value Ta Sy Meaning
.It Dv EVENTLOG_SESSION_CREATE_ID Ta Li 0xFFFFFFFE Ta Session created
.It Dv EVENTLOG_SESSION_END_ID Ta Li 0xFFFFFFFF Ta Session destroyed
.It Dv EVENTLOG_DUMP_COMPLETE_ID Ta Li 0xFFFFFFFD Ta Dump-state replay finished
.El
.Pp
Two further
.Va event_id
values
.Pq Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_INFO_ID / Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_BLOB_ID ,
aliased in
.Xr elog 1
as
.Dv ELOG_EVENT_PROVIDER_*_ID
are reserved for provider-announcement bookkeeping.
They are synthesised by the kernel at late bind and also by
.Xr elog 1
when writing a binary file; see
.Sx Provider Announcement Records :
.Bl -column "ELOG_EVENT_PROVIDER_INFO_ID" "0xFFFFFFFC" -offset indent
.It Sy Constant Ta Sy Value Ta Sy Meaning
.It Dv ELOG_EVENT_PROVIDER_INFO_ID Ta Li 0xFFFFFFFC Ta Provider-table entry, embedded mid-stream
.It Dv ELOG_EVENT_PROVIDER_BLOB_ID Ta Li 0xFFFFFFFB Ta Decode blob, embedded mid-stream
.El
.Pp
The
.Dv EVENTLOG_SESSION_END_ID
and
.Dv EVENTLOG_DUMP_COMPLETE_ID
events have an empty payload
.Pq 0 bytes .
All other payload formats are provider-specific and defined by schema files.
The payload size is
.Va event_length
minus 32.
.Pp
Identity/metadata events
.Po
.Dv EVENTLOG_SESSION_CREATE_ID No and Dv EVENTLOG_DUMP_COMPLETE_ID
.Pc
and provider-specific state such as the
.Li symbols
provider's
.Li USER_FILE
or the
.Li process
provider's
.Li PROC_EXEC
are not guaranteed to appear only once per capture.
The
.Fl -roll-time
mode of
.Xr elog 1
re-emits them at every rolled-file boundary so each file decodes
independently; a parser must treat a repeat sighting of the same key
.Pq e.g. Va session_id No or a provider's own identity key
as replacing the earlier one, not as an error or a duplicate to reject.
.Pp
The event stream may end with an incomplete event at EOF if the capture
was interrupted.
Parsers should stop when fewer than 32 bytes remain or when
.Va event_length
exceeds the remaining data.
.Ss Provider Announcement Records
The provider table and decode-blob section
.Pq Sx Provider Table , Sx Decode Blob Section
are written once, when a file is opened, from the set of providers the
kernel reports at that moment
.Pq via Dv EVENTLOG_IOCTL_GET_PROVIDERS2 .
A provider that registers with
.Xr eventlog 9
later in the capture is not in that snapshot.
The kernel synthesises an
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_INFO_ID
record
.Pq and, when the decode blob fits in one event, an Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_BLOB_ID
record
into every subscriber that just bound the provider, so the identity is in
the stream even if the provider unregisters before the next poll.
.Xr elog 1
applies those records to its provider map and decode registry and embeds
them into the output file; it also synthesises the same records itself the
first time it is about to write an event from a provider not already
covered by the file's provider table or by an earlier announcement in this
same file
.Pq belt-and-suspenders for rolled-file re-announcement and older kernels .
Both use the ordinary event header; a reader that does not know about them
can safely skip them like any other unrecognized
.Va event_id .
.Pp
An
.Dv ELOG_EVENT_PROVIDER_INFO_ID
record's payload is a single packed
.Vt struct eventlog_provider_info
.Pq the same 50-byte layout as a provider table entry; see Sx Provider Table :
.Bl -column "Offset" "Size" "uint16_t" "provider_id" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Size Ta Sy Type Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 2 Ta Vt uint16_t Ta Va provider_id
.It 2 Ta 32 Ta Vt char[32] Ta Va name
.It 34 Ta 16 Ta Vt uint8_t[16] Ta Va schema_id
.El
.Pp
A reader applies it exactly as it would a provider table entry: upserting
by
.Va provider_id
into its provider map.
.Pp
An
.Dv ELOG_EVENT_PROVIDER_BLOB_ID
record's payload is a
.Va schema_id ,
a 4-byte blob size, and that many raw decode-blob bytes, the same layout as
one decode-blob section entry
.Pq see Sx Decode Blob Section :
.Bl -column "Size" "uint8_t[16]" "schema_id" -offset indent
.It Sy Size Ta Sy Type Ta Sy Field
.It 16 Ta Vt uint8_t[16] Ta Va schema_id
.It 4 Ta Vt uint32_t Ta Va blob_size
.It Va blob_size Ta Vt uint8_t[] Ta Va blob
.El
.Pp
The record's total size is bounded by
.Va event_length
.Pq a Vt uint16_t covering the whole event ,
so a blob too large to fit is not embedded this way: elog(1) instead warns
and leaves that provider's events to be decoded from the live kernel by
.Va schema_id
on replay, the same fallback used when a file was written with
.Fl N .
No provider's decode blob is expected to approach this limit in practice.
.Pp
Both records are idempotent per output file: elog(1) writes each provider
at most once and each schema at most once per file, and a reader that sees
a repeat sighting of the same
.Va provider_id
or
.Va schema_id
.Pq e.g. across rolled files
treats it as replacing the earlier one, per the general rule in
.Sx Event Stream .
Embedding this way does not change the file format version; the records
are additive to version 3 and an old
.Xr elog 1
that predates them still decodes the rest of the file correctly, simply
without the late-registering provider's events.
.Ss Compressed Files
The
.Xr elog 1
utility also accepts
.Pa .gz Ns -compressed
files
.Pq detected by file extension .
The decompressed content has the same format described above.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Capture TCP events to a binary file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -c tcp -o /tmp/events.elog
.Ed
.Pp
Read back as formatted text:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -r /tmp/events.elog
.Ed
.Pp
Read a compressed capture:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
elog -r /tmp/events.elog.gz
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr elog 1 ,
.Xr eventlog 9
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
file format first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
